AB  - The localization, identification, and segmentation of spinal structures are critical foundations for intelligent diagnosis and treatment planning, with multi-class segmentation of vertebrae and intervertebral discs being particularly important. However, due to the similarity in gray-scale values between vertebral edges and surrounding tissues, coupled with the inherent blurriness of boundary regions, existing spinal segmentation methods still exhibit limitations in terms of fine-grained segmentation in boundary regions. To address this issue, we propose a Dual Encoder Spinal Segmentation Network (DESS-Net). In this paper, we introduce a frequency-domain Transformer that utilizes Haar wavelet decomposition for multi-band information extraction, enabling the capture of high-frequency details and low-frequency structural features in spinal images. Additionally, to further enhance fine-grained features in spinal boundary regions, we propose a multi-scale semantic difference module (MSDM). This module is diffusion-guided to better locate boundaries between two anatomical structures, where the EID kernel carries explicit kernel implicit difference information, providing more accurate boundary cues for subsequent segmentation tasks. Comparative and ablation experiments conducted on two datasets demonstrate that DESS-Net outperforms existing state-of-the-art models. On the CT dataset, the average dice similarity coefficient for segmentation reaches 84.54%, and on the MR dataset, it reaches 83.11%.
