AB  - Binary semantic masks (BSMs) represent a type of structured discrete data that describe the spatial distribution of target and background regions. As critical outputs of on-board AI processing in remote sensing, transmitting these data securely and efficiently over constrained channels poses a significant challenge, since traditional methods struggle to balance high compression ratios (CRs) with robust data protection. To address this problem, this article proposes a framework for the secure and efficient lossless compression of BSMs, named joint encryption and lossless compression framework for (JELCF)-BSM. The method first exploits spatial redundancy by segmenting the data into homogeneous blocks (HB) and non-HB, followed by encryption via block-level scrambling and diffusion. Subsequently, a modified 2-D binary discrete cosine transform is designed to establish a one-to-one correspondence between binary blocks and transform-domain DC coefficients. Based on this unique representation, an index coding strategy is developed to achieve efficient lossless compression of encrypted semantic masks. Experimental results demonstrate that JELCF-BSM achieves an average CR approximately 82% higher than conventional lossless compression schemes when compressing encrypted BSMs, and 56% higher than compared joint encryption and compression schemes. Security evaluations further confirm that the method exhibits strong robustness against statistical and differential attacks. This method provides a feasible solution for secure and efficient remote sensing semantic data transmission.
