TI  - MimiGen: Generative mimicry attacks against provenance-based intrusion detection systems
T2  - EXPERT SYSTEMS WITH APPLICATIONS
LA  - English
KW  - Provenance graph
KW  - Mimicry attack
KW  - Intrusion detection system
KW  - Adversarial example
AB  - Provenance-based Intrusion Detection Systems (PIDSs) are becoming increasingly crucial for detecting potential security incidents by leveraging provenance graphs to monitor system entities and their associated events. Recently, mimicry attacks have been employed to uncover the weaknesses of PIDSs, tampering with malicious graphs to disguise them as benign by injecting entities (nodes) or modifying events (edges). However, previous approaches have several shortcomings: (1) failing to extract the rich structural information contained within benign graphs, (2) diminishing the subtlety of the mimicry attacks due to the injection of numerous nodes, and (3) disrupting the original attack paths in a malicious graph during the modification process. In this paper, we introduce MimiGen, a graph-level mimicry attack that generates adversarial graphs from malicious ones to achieve a high evasion rate with suitable perturbation. MimiGen leverages a graph diffusion model to capture the structural and feature distinctions between benign graphs and their anomaly subgraphs, enabling deeper structural information extraction from benign graphs. It then injects a set of nodes and edges (named "concealer"), carefully constructed by the model, into the malicious graph while preserving the original attack paths. Before injection, MimiGen determines the appropriate binding sites and concealer size based on the malicious graph to enhance the concealer's subtlety. We evaluate MimiGen on four datasets and against four PIDSs. The results show that MimiGen can generate evasion graphs with small perturbations and outperform the state-of-the-art mimicry attacks. The source code is available at https://github.com/zyjOwO/mimigen.
