TI  - 9D fractional-order memristive neural network with hidden attractors for secure remote sensing image encryption
T2  - PHYSICA SCRIPTA
LA  - English
KW  - discrete fractional
KW  - memristor
KW  - chialvo neuron
KW  - hidden attractor
KW  - hyperchaos
KW  - image encryption
AB  - To address the limitations of low-dimensional discrete chaotic systems regarding key space and complexity, and to incorporate memory effects that enhance system unpredictability, this paper proposes a novel 9-dimensional discrete fractional-order memristive Chialvo ring network (9D-DFMCRN). Constructed using the Caputo-like difference operator, the model couples three discrete fractional-order Chialvo neurons in a unidirectional ring topology via locally active memristors. Through theoretical analysis of fixed-point stability, the phenomenon of coexistence between stable fixed points and chaotic attractors under specific parameters is revealed, confirming the existence of hidden attractors. Furthermore, numerical tools such as phase portraits, Lyapunov exponent spectra, and sample entropy are employed to comprehensively validate the system's hyperchaotic characteristics, strong memory effects, and high sequence complexity. Leveraging the high-quality pseudo-random sequences generated by the 9D-DFMCRN, an image encryption algorithm adopting a 'Block-Diffusion-Confusion' architecture is designed. This algorithm integrates bidirectional alternating diffusion, bit-level circular shifting, Pseudo-Hadamard Transform (PHT), and global scrambling strategies. Extensive security analyses-including histogram statistics, correlation analysis, information entropy, and NPCR and UACI tests-demonstrate that the proposed scheme possesses a vast key space and effectively resists statistical, differential, noise, and cropping attacks, making it highly suitable for high-security image communication.
