TI  - Actuator-aware diffusion motion planning for hyper-redundant tendon-driven continuum robots
T2  - ROBOTICS AND AUTONOMOUS SYSTEMS
LA  - English
KW  - Tendon-driven continuum robot
KW  - Motion planning
KW  - Diffusion model
KW  - Actuator-aware planning
KW  - Trajectory generation
KW  - DESIGN
KW  - SYSTEM
AB  - Hyper-redundant tendon-driven continuum robots (HTDCRs) are well suited for manipulation tasks in narrow and cluttered environments, but their motion planning must handle coupled constraints across actuator, joint, and task spaces. Conventional geometric planning usually first searches for collision-free paths and then relies on inverse kinematics or low-level control for execution, which may produce trajectories that are geometrically feasible but violate actuator constraints, including tendon-displacement, tendon-velocity, and tendon-acceleration limits. This paper proposes an actuator-aware diffusion motion planning framework. The method generates multiple candidate joint trajectories in the B-spline control-point space and introduces multi-space gradient guidance during denoising to jointly account for whole-body obstacle avoidance, actuator feasibility, and target-pose satisfaction. Under the small-angle assumption supported by the robot design, the tendon Jacobian is approximated as a constant matrix, enabling efficient backpropagation of actuator constraints to B-spline control points. Simulation experiments compare the proposed method with sampling-based planners, a pointwise IK baseline, a trajectory-optimization baseline, and an external diffusion-planning baseline. Results show that the proposed method maintains high task success rates while generating shorter, smoother, and executable trajectories, and substantially reduces tendon-velocity violations observed in conventional and discrete-diffusion baselines. Ablation studies, actuator-model validation, and confined passage experiments further validate the multi-space guidance, the constant-Jacobian approximation, and the adaptability to complex environments. Experiments on a six-section HTDCR prototype show that the generated trajectories can be executed on the physical robot.
