AB  - Discrete diffusion semigroups have proven to be highly effective tools for machine learning and data analysis due to the interplay between diffusion processes (of inferences), and their naturally associated geometries. Inspired by the harmonic analysis machinery that E. Stein developed for symmetric diffusion semigroups acting on L-p spaces, we show that a correspondence between the rate of diffusion approximation and a Besov-type version of smoothness exists in the general continuous case, even without a local kernel representation. Specifically, let {At}(t >= 0) be a symmetric diffusion semigroup on L-p(X), for X a complete positive sigma-finite measure space. We first establish that for 1 < p < infinity the semigroup {A(t)}(t >= 0) acting on L-p(X) is strongly continuous and holomorphic. We then show that for f is an element of L-p(X) and 0 < alpha < 1, the following are equivalent:
   1) parallel to(A(t) - I) f parallel to(Lp)(X) = ct(alpha), for 0 < t <= 1;
   (2) vertical bar < f, h >vertical bar <= c integral(1)(0) s(alpha) parallel to A(s)h parallel to(Lp') (X) ds/s, for all h is an element of L-p' (X); and
   (3) parallel to AA(t) f parallel to(Lp)(X) <= ct(alpha-1), for 0 < t <= 1, where A is the infinitesimal generator.
   We also present some extensions, including results for the p = infinity case.
