AB  - This paper addresses data-driven modeling and control of spatio-temporal dynamics in biomechanical systems by combining Bayesian neural networks (BNNs) with classical control theory. We consider discrete diffusion-type neural network representations of mechanical motion with time-varying delays, motivated in particular by human gait kinematics and related mechatronic applications such as prosthetic or exoskeleton assistance. To identify the distributed coupling structure and time-delay effects from measured motion data, we develop an enhanced sparse Bayesian learning scheme for spatio-temporal BNNs. The method employs an adaptive learning rate strategy for hyperparameter updates together with a Laplacian-regularized hierarchical prior that enforces spatial smoothness while retaining parameter sparsity, thus improving identifiability, numerical robustness and convergence in practical engineering use. On top of the learned dynamic model, a proportional-integral-derivative controller is embedded in the slave system of a master-slave architecture to achieve accurate tracking and synchronization of mechanical motion. To support controller analysis and implementation, a tailored Lyapunov-Krasovskii functional is constructed, from which tractable linear matrix inequality conditions are derived to verify global exponential synchronization of the corresponding nominal error system, providing a rigorous and implementable analysis tool for the kinematics, dynamics and control of delayed mechanical systems. The overall framework is validated on human gait kinematics data, demonstrating effective oscillation suppression, elimination of steady-state error and reliable recovery of spatio-temporal interaction patterns. The results highlight how advanced artificial intelligence (AI) techniques-specifically sparse BNNs- can be systematically integrated with the proposed controller to solve real-world problems in machine science, bioengineering and intelligent mechanical systems.
