AB  - Ammonia recovery from anaerobic digestates is a key strategy for nutrient valorization within circular economy frameworks, yet these high-strength streams are challenging for membrane technologies, and practical guidance on their treatment remains scarce. This study addresses this gap by directly comparing two commercial membrane diffusion technologies-direct contact membrane distillation (DCMD) and liquid-liquid membrane contactors (LLMC)-for ammonia recovery from brewery digestate. Both rely on vapor pressure-driven separation but differ greatly in membrane material (e.g., PTFE vs PMP) structure (e.g., pore size and structure, hydrophobicity, thickness) and module design (e.g., flat plate vs hollow fibre). The PTFE-DCMD module demonstrated high robustness, enabling stable operation over 21 days with limited cleaning, though water vapor transport led to permeate dilution. During this period, an average ammonia removal of 58.4 % +/- 8.9 % was achieved, producing a permeate TAN concentration of 13.16 g L--(1) (maximum 15 g L--(1)) and an overall concentration factor of 3.5 from an initial 3.6 g TAN L-(1). Approximately 278 L of ammonium sulfate solution were recovered-a 7.5-fold volume reduction-containing similar to 3.4 kg TAN out of similar to 4.8 kg available (similar to 70 % recovery). In contrast, the PMP-LLMC showed higher initial ammonia flux but suffered from severe fouling, suspected partial wetting, and irreversible performance loss. The study shows that conventional metrics (e.g., concentration factor) can misrepresent outcomes and introduces normalized flux indicators that better capture selectivity and overall performance. The results demonstrate that membrane and module characteristics jointly determine process feasibility and offer practical guidance for scaling nutrient recovery from high-strength wastewaters.
