TI  - The diffusion of artificial intelligence methods in scientific research: growth pattern, disciplinary variation, and task alignment
T2  - JOURNAL OF INFORMETRICS
LA  - English
KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - Bibliometric analysis
KW  - Research method
KW  - Research lifecycle
KW  - Network analysis
KW  - AI application
KW  - EMERGING TECHNOLOGIES
KW  - BEHAVIOR
KW  - MODELS
AB  - How AI methods are integrated into scientific research remains poorly understood despite the rapid growth of AI adoption in science. This study provides large-scale empirical documentation of this integration, analyzing 27,636,491 Web of Science publications from 2000 to 2022. Using a task-oriented AI method classification system from Papers with Code, a three-round LLM-based identification pipeline, bibliometric analysis, co-occurrence network analysis, and an entropyweighted TOPSIS evaluation framework, we identify 361,480 AI application papers spanning 867 distinct methods. Three principal patterns emerge. First, the share of publications employing AI methods almost tripled between 2018 and 2022, with AI present in at least 120 WoS subfields by the end of the period. Second, adoption rates vary by more than an order of magnitude across disciplines (from approximately 9% in Engineering sciences to below 1.5% in the Humanities sciences), a pattern consistent with differences in disciplinary knowledge forms. Third, among application papers referencing multiple AI methods, the study reveals a task-level specialization across a four-stage research lifecycle: interpretable classical methods are associated with earlystage knowledge acquisition and hypothesis construction, while deep learning methods are most prominent at experimental validation, and specialist optimization methods concentrate at result generation and transformation. These findings characterize AI integration as a process of functional stratification, and suggest the pre-2022 methodological ecosystem formed a critical foundation for the emergence of generative AI in science.
