AB  - The treatment of NH4+-N in high-strength pharmaceutical wastewater remains a significant challenge due to the inefficiency of conventional methods at concentrations exceeding 5000 mg/L NH4+-N. This work presents, for the first time, a comprehensive investigation into the efficacy and underlying mechanisms of a polypropylene (PP) hollow-fiber liquid-liquid membrane contactor (HF-LLMC) for ammonia removal from real pharmaceutical wastewater with unprecedented NH4+-N concentrations up to 13,900 mg/L. The effect of key operating parameters, including feed temperature, pH, flow rate, and initial NH4+-N concentration, absorbent type, concentration, and flow rate, were systematically evaluated. Among the four flow configurations tested, the horizontal countercurrent mode achieved the highest ammonia removal efficiency of 94.5%. Optimal performance was identified at a feed pH of 11-12, a temperature of 50 degrees C, and a feed flow rate of 5-10 mL/min, using sulfuric acid (8-10 wt%) as the absorbent. The analysis revealed that the process was governed by feed-side boundary layer diffusion coupled with rapid interfacial protonation, which maintained a high driving force even at extreme loads, resulting in consistent removal efficiencies of 88-94.5%. A duplicate 12 h stability test under the optimized conditions further maintained stable performance, reaching 93.5% NH4+-N removal with a residual concentration of approximately 390 mg/L. This study establishes a mechanistic framework and verifies that HF-LLMC technology is suitable for treating high-strength industrial wastewater.
